d. Flagged for human\n   verification.\n4. **Platform Operator has no architecture-context file**: it is treated as\n   in-platform because it appears in the canonical RHAI component list and the\n   strategy premise is remediation of its existing images (not provisioning).\n   No onboarding chain was generated. Signal 9 (architecture_claims) is\n   scored 0 for its epic for this reason; verify its repository and pipeline\n   status before starting.\n5. **External schedule gates are risks, not ACs**: the accredited-lab\n   turnaround (historically six to nine months) and security-review-board\n   cadence are externally controlled and cannot be compressed by engineering\n   effort. They are recorded as risks on the evidence epic; its ACs stop at\n   the engineering completion point (evidence package assembled and submitted\n   through the review board to the lab, accepted into review). The\n   certification grant itself is outside epic scope.\n6. **Bundled lower-priority HLR**: HLR-3 (P1) is bundled into the P0\n   remediation epics per the shared-surface enforcement rule — rationale\n   recorded in the HLR Traceability Matrix row above.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nThe external dependency with uncertain timing here is the certification\nprocess itself (accredited lab, six-to-nine-month historical turnaround), not\nan upstream code contribution — no upstream PR/RFC epic is generated (Rule 21\ndoes not apply). Partial value is deliverable without the certification\noutcome, so the decomposition tiers naturally:\n\n- **Tier 1 (independent value)**: HLR-1 + HLR-3 — the serving-stack images\n  run correctly on FIPS-enabled clusters with validated crypto, enforced by\n  gating CI. This is user-facing value (E001, E002) that does not wait on the\n  lab.\n- **Tier 2 (process-gated)**: HLR-2 — the evidence package is assembled from\n  the remediated images and submitted (E003); the certification grant arrives\n  on the lab's schedule, which is outside engineering control.\n\nNo 