{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-96c6642147aae6e5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9013: FIPS 140-3 compliance for the model serving stack\n11\t\n12\tTriage: full decomposition (not below-threshold, not docs-only). The strategy\n13\tspans multiple components (KServe, Platform Operator) and multiple distinct\n14\twork streams (crypto-library remediation code changes, compliance evidence\n15\tpackage assembly and submission, substantive documentation authoring), so the\n16\tbelow-threshold escape condition for multiple distinct work streams applies.\n17\t\n18\tStaff Engineer Input: absent from the strategy — no overrides to log.\n19\t\n20\t## Epic List\n21\t\n22\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n23\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 | KServe FIPS 140-3 image remediation and FIPS regression CI enforcement | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 | Platform Operator FIPS 140-3 image remediation and FIPS regression CI enforcement | Implementation | RHOAI Platform | P0 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 | Serving-stack FIPS 140-3 compliance evidence package and certification submission | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 | Document the FIPS support posture and cluster prerequisites for the model serving stack | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n28\t\n29\tNote on E004 priority: HLR-4 is P2 in the strategy, but `docs-authoring` epics\n30\tderive priority from the strategy frontmatter (`priority: Critical` → P0) per\n31\tthe docs-authoring priority exception; docs edges do not push priority\n32\tupstream (the P0 priorities of E001–E003 come from HLR-1/HLR-2).\n33\t\n34\t## Dependency DAG\n35\t\n36\t```mermaid\n37\tgraph TD\n38\t    E001[\"E001 KServe FIPS image remediation + CI\"] --> E003[\"E003 Compliance evidence package + submission\"]\n39\t    E002[\"E002 Platform Operator FIPS image remediation + CI\"] --> E003\n40\t    E001 --> E004[\"E004 FIPS posture documentation\"]\n41\t    E002 --> E004\n42\t    E003 --> E004\n43\t```\n44\t\n45\t## DAG Justification\n46\t\n47\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n48\t|------|------|-----------|\n49\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 13 (artifact consumption) | The evidence package is assembled from the module inventory, build attestations, and test results of the final remediated KServe images; attesting pre-remediation images would produce invalid evidence, so the evidence epic consumes the remediation epics' outputs and is serial after them. |\n50\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 13 (artifact consumption) | Same rationale for the Platform Operator image(s): its final builds' attestations and test results are inputs to the evidence package. |\n51\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation states the FIPS support posture that the remediation establishes; blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy. |\n52\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Same — Platform Operator remediation is part of the posture being documented. |\n53\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | The documented posture must reflect the submitted evidence package, not a plan. |\n54\t| No edge E001 ↔ E002 | Rule 14 (parallel) | Different components and repositories with no shared artifacts; the two remediations proceed in parallel. |\n55\t| No standalone CI-enforcement epic | Step 5 shared-surface rule | HLR-3's gating FIPS-regression CI check is an enforcement deliverable that applies across the same surfaces being converted (the serving-stack images). It is divided per surface: each remediation epic delivers and verifies the regression check for its own component's builds (E001 for KServe builds, E002 for Platform Operator builds). A standalone CI epic would carve one logical change into a work-type epic and leave each remediation epic's slice incomplete. |\n56\t| No onboarding chain | Rule 20 (not applicable) | Neither component is new: KServe is in the platform (architecture context `architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`, Konflux-onboarded) and Platform Operator is an established canonical RHAI component whose existing images the strategy remediates. No repo/konflux onboarding epics are generated; see Health Warnings for the Platform Operator context-file gap. |\n57\t| No Investigation epics | Step 3 decision rule | The strategy's open-questions table states \"None on scope\". The remaining unknowns (which images link which non-validated crypto libraries, and how to remediate each) do not change which epics exist or what they do — remediation proceeds the same way regardless, and a failed attempt is fix-and-retry. They are captured as scope/ACs on the remediation epics (module inventory per image is part of each epic's deliverable). Lab scheduling and review-board cadence are externally controlled schedule constraints, not unknowns. |\n58\t\n59\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n60\t\n61\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n62\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n63\t| HLR-1 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9013-E001, RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 | \"All serving-stack images\" splits along the component boundary (Rule 1): KServe's own images (including its serving data plane) in E001, the Platform Operator image(s) in E002. Both epics are P0 directly and as prerequisite epics of the P0 evidence epic. |\n64\t| HLR-2 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 | Evidence package (module inventory, build attestations, test results), submission through the security review board to the external certification lab. A distinct work stream (compliance artifact assembly vs. code remediation) with different review criteria, so it is its own epic rather than ACs on the remediation epics. |\n65\t| HLR-3 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 (bundled), RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 (bundled) | Bundled, not split: the CI FIPS-regression check is an enforcement deliverable over the exact same surfaces (serving-stack builds) that E001/E002 convert — it shares the builds, pipelines, and module-inventory data with the P0 remediation and could not ship as an independent epic with user value (a check over unconverted builds fails by definition). Per the shared-surface enforcement rule each surface epic authors and verifies the check's coverage for its own component's builds; a standalone epic would fragment one logical change and force a second pass over the same build configurations. |\n66\t| HLR-4 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 | Substantive new documentation topics (FIPS support posture, cluster prerequisites), a distinct work-product stream → separate `docs-authoring` epic. Priority P0 via the docs-authoring exception (strategy `priority: Critical`), not via inheritance. |\n67\t\n68\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n69\t\n70\t## Health Warnings\n71\t\n72\t1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted\n73\t   Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria,\n74\t   and no Non-Functional Requirements section; Affected Components is a bare\n75\t   list with no change/owner detail. Decomposition proceeded from the\n76\t   available information.\n77\t2. **Team assignments are assumptions**: only KServe's owner (RHOAI Serving)\n78\t   is grounded, in `architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`. The Platform Operator\n79\t   epic is assigned to a placeholder owning team (\"RHOAI Platform\"), and the\n80\t   evidence/documentation epics to the serving-stack owner — confirm with the\n81\t   engagement before scheduling.\n82\t3. **\"Serving data plane\" scope ambiguity**: the problem statement says\n83\t   \"KServe components and the serving data plane\" link non-validated crypto,\n84\t   but Affected Components lists only KServe and Platform Operator. Per the\n85\t   KServe architecture context, model execution in the data plane is provided\n86\t   by Serving Runtimes (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit). If the non-validated crypto\n87\t   lives in those runtime images, the strategy may be under-scoped — a\n88\t   follow-up strategy or scope amendment would be needed. Flagged for human\n89\t   verification.\n90\t4. **Platform Operator has no architecture-context file**: it is treated as\n91\t   in-platform because it appears in the canonical RHAI component list and the\n92\t   strategy premise is remediation of its existing images (not provisioning).\n93\t   No onboarding chain was generated. Signal 9 (architecture_claims) is\n94\t   scored 0 for its epic for this reason; verify its repository and pipeline\n95\t   status before starting.\n96\t5. **External schedule gates are risks, not ACs**: the accredited-lab\n97\t   turnaround (historically six to nine months) and security-review-board\n98\t   cadence are externally controlled and cannot be compressed by engineering\n99\t   effort. They are recorded as risks on the evidence epic; its ACs stop at\n100\t   the engineering completion point (evidence package assembled and submitted\n101\t   through the review board to the lab, accepted into review). The\n102\t   certification grant itself is outside epic scope.\n103\t6. **Bundled lower-priority HLR**: HLR-3 (P1) is bundled into the P0\n104\t   remediation epics per the shared-surface enforcement rule — rationale\n105\t   recorded in the HLR Traceability Matrix row above.\n106\t\n107\t## Tiered Delivery\n108\t\n109\tThe external dependency with uncertain timing here is the certification\n110\tprocess itself (accredited lab, six-to-nine-month historical turnaround), not\n111\tan upstream code contribution — no upstream PR/RFC epic is generated (Rule 21\n112\tdoes not apply). Partial value is deliverable without the certification\n113\toutcome, so the decomposition tiers naturally:\n114\t\n115\t- **Tier 1 (independent value)**: HLR-1 + HLR-3 — the serving-stack images\n116\t  run correctly on FIPS-enabled clusters with validated crypto, enforced by\n117\t  gating CI. This is user-facing value (E001, E002) that does not wait on the\n118\t  lab.\n119\t- **Tier 2 (process-gated)**: HLR-2 — the evidence package is assembled from\n120\t  the remediated images and submitted (E003); the certification grant arrives\n121\t  on the lab's schedule, which is outside engineering control.\n122\t\n123\tNo conditional branches: the strategy has no bounded-outcome unknowns.\n124\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9af509d349c0416a","agent_id":"af22f844fbe11d9c9","session_id":"58d4774e-cbd2-4ab5-97a3-4f58877fb394","uuid":"84ee1207-475f-4947-8f2c-d35ac909c6a1","timestamp":"2026-10-26T11:21:27.478Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-96c6642147aae6e5","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9013 decomposition"}
